WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.602
-Our next guests
are the writers, producers,

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.938
and stars of the new movie
"Dicks: The Musical,"

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:06.372
which is in theaters
everywhere now.

00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:07.607
Let's take a look.

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:10.643
-This is crazy, right?
-Totally crazy.

00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:11.911
I mean, what are the odds

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:13.246
of working with
your long-lost twin?

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:16.048
-I don't know. 1 in 4, 3 in 4?
It's crazy.

00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:18.417
-Crazy. But wait...

00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:20.486
I can't believe Mom and Dad
split us up.

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:23.189
Like, is that abuse?
Did they abuse us?

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.191
-We definitely
experienced "abusement."

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.626
-Oh, my God.
-I mean, don't get me wrong.

00:00:27.626 --> 00:00:29.228
My life is awesome.
-No, no, no, no, no.

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:30.897
M-M-Mine is awesome, too.

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.599
-I have an amazing job.
-Amazing job.

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.100
-Always dating
some big [Bleep] lady.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.836
-Stunning big [Bleep] lady.
-I always get the things I want.

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:39.705
Except for a real family.
-Single-parent homes

00:00:39.705 --> 00:00:42.008
are not real families.
That's true.

00:00:42.008 --> 00:00:44.009
Hmm.
-Hmm.

00:00:44.009 --> 00:00:47.013
-What do we do?

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:50.583
-Please welcome to the show
Aaron Jackson and Josh Sharp,

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:51.884
everybody!

00:00:51.884 --> 00:00:54.253
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.253 --> 00:00:57.457
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:00:57.457 --> 00:01:06.298
♪♪

00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:08.267
Welcome to the show,
gentlemen.

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:09.869
-Thank you.
-And congratulations

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:11.304
on this incredible film.

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:12.938
We've been lucky to have
some of your costars.

00:01:12.938 --> 00:01:15.575
Bowen Yang was here,
the great Nathan Lane was here.

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:17.009
-Uh-huh.
-Yes.

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:18.344
-And then there are
some other --

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:19.812
Mega Mullally's in it.
Megan Thee Stallion is in it.

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:21.914
-Yeah, Thee Stallion
and Thee Mullally.

00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:24.016
-Yeah, really, uh --
-You almost have,

00:01:24.016 --> 00:01:25.852
like, the royal flush
of the whole cast.

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:27.053
-You did an incredible job.

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:29.021
It must be thrilling
to get people like that

00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:31.558
to do this
very singular piece of work.

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:33.793
-Sure. That's a nice way
to say "crazy."

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:36.729
-[ Laughs ]
-So it is so deeply crazy.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.864
Nathan Lane, who's done
so many crazy things,

00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:42.034
basically said it's the craziest
thing he's ever done.

00:01:42.034 --> 00:01:44.303
-A badge of honor
to hear that from Daddy.

00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:46.472
-Yeah, truly,
we wear that with pride.

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:50.042
-So, this is a show you guys
used to do onstage at UCB?

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:51.311
-Yes.
-True.

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:53.412
-And I don't want to give
too much of the plot away,

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:56.215
but it is sort of
a "Parent Trap"-type film.

00:01:56.215 --> 00:01:58.485
-It's true. We're using
"The Parent Trap" as a device

00:01:58.485 --> 00:02:01.987
because it's really one of the
oldest story structures we have.

00:02:01.987 --> 00:02:03.123
There's, like,
"Romeo and Juliet,"

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:04.924
"The Odyssey,"
and "The Parent Trap."

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:07.893
They're sort of accepted
narrative modalities.

00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:09.762
-And you could see
from that clip,

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:13.365
we're sort of -- we're playing
heterosexual men.

00:02:13.365 --> 00:02:16.102
But we are not heterosexual men.
-We're not.

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:19.272
-So it's actually
very brave and...

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:21.107
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:23.109
Yeah! Thank you.

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:25.778
-In fact, the opening
of the film,

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:27.780
just in case -- you're
not breaking news here,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.315
because I believe
the opening of the film

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:31.084
has a title card
saying how brave it is.

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:33.719
-We want Oscars bad.
-Yes.

00:02:33.719 --> 00:02:35.154
-And this is
how you win an Oscar.

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:36.923
-I believe you say
it's the first film

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:40.226
ever written by a gay writer.
-Yes.

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.727
-I know your show
has researchers.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:45.164
We don't, so if that is wrong,
don't tell on us.

00:02:45.164 --> 00:02:46.899
-Not on us.
-Not on us.

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:48.234
How do we do, playing straight?

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:50.469
-Oh, my God,
it's so believable.

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:54.407
-Does it feel lived in?
-It felt very great. Thank you.

00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:56.509
It was so nice to see,
finally,

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.110
it's, like, see
myself represented.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.079
-Yeah, totally.
-You know what I mean?

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:03.716
-I know. I lived as straight
for like 18 years.

00:03:03.716 --> 00:03:05.551
So it was Method,
the work I had done.

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:08.721
-It showed up.
It really showed up on-screen.

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:10.923
-You call them
"big [Bleep] ladies," right?

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:13.025
-We do.
-That's an industry term?

00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:15.294
-We do -- industry, the fellas.

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:17.997
I mean, you -- of the many
things you had to do,

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:21.867
you actually wrote a rap and
then had to send the lyrics...

00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:23.937
-Yes.
-...to Megan Thee Stallion.

00:03:23.937 --> 00:03:26.705
-She's -- She's rapped before.
-Yeah.

00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:29.075
And she's rather good at it.
-Yeah.

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:31.343
-A lot of people say it's one
of the things she's known for.

00:03:31.343 --> 00:03:34.012
-Yeah.
-I would say, top 4 or 5 things.

00:03:34.012 --> 00:03:37.216
But then our gay asses
wrote a bunch of words,

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:38.751
and we're like,
"She won't say them."

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:40.986
And then she rapped
every single one of those words.

00:03:40.986 --> 00:03:43.822
-There's, like --
There's amazing footage of Josh

00:03:43.822 --> 00:03:45.725
just, like, rapping it
into a cellphone

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:47.093
to, like, send to her manager.

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:49.328
And then she just listened
to that rap

00:03:49.328 --> 00:03:50.996
and was like,
"Great, I'll do it."

00:03:50.996 --> 00:03:53.299
And when she showed up on-set,
she was like --

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:55.101
we were rehearsing --
she was like,

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:56.702
"I just want to say,
people say I'm dirty,

00:03:56.702 --> 00:03:59.439
but y'all are dirty."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:02.441
-Profound badge of honor
to have Miss The Stallion

00:04:02.441 --> 00:04:06.512
say, "You have done me right.
This is dirty."

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:08.114
-Do you have trepidation
when you send --

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:09.715
when you record a rap?
-Well, I was told

00:04:09.715 --> 00:04:11.718
it would just go
to her manager and not her.

00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:13.452
So I was hoodwinked.
And then she showed up

00:04:13.452 --> 00:04:15.088
and was like,
"You got bars, young man."

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:17.490
-Yeah.
-So, what did you think?

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.559
Did you think the manager
would then rap it to her?

00:04:20.559 --> 00:04:22.528
-I don't know what I thought.
I thought it was just like --

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:25.464
Really, we kept expecting her
to be -- to say no.

00:04:25.464 --> 00:04:27.233
Every point, I was like,
"Someone will hear this

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.101
and go, "She's out.
She can't possibly do this."

00:04:29.101 --> 00:04:30.703
-It's really wonderful,
because, you know --

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:32.805
and it's very early --
when she shows up,

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:36.642
she shows up with an energy
where she's like, "I am game.

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:38.177
I am in on this.
-She's so cool.

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:40.579
I mean, she was amazing.
-And now, Nathan Lane

00:04:40.579 --> 00:04:43.616
talked about his scene partners
are the Sewer Boys.

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:46.051
I don't want to give too much
away about the Sewer Boys,

00:04:46.051 --> 00:04:47.453
but he did bring them
on the show.

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:48.888
-Yeah.
-The Sewer Boys are

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:53.826
sort of a subterranean creatures
that he adopted

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:56.529
and keeps in his apartment.
-So beautiful and also pretty.

00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:58.364
Gorgeous.
-Can I correct the record?

00:04:58.364 --> 00:05:00.065
Everyone, including
on this program,

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:02.768
has called them puppets.
These are actors that we cast.

00:05:02.768 --> 00:05:05.905
-Yeah, they came in,
they auditioned for the role.

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:07.373
They just brought something.
-They did a great --

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:09.175
So, Nathan brought them
to the show,

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:11.210
and then they actually
stuck around our offices.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.213
-Yeah, they work hard.
-Hire them for admin.

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:16.482
-Hire them for admin
or for acting.

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:17.783
-They're so good at admin,
though.

00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:19.685
-They are, they are.

00:05:19.685 --> 00:05:23.489
-We read Chalamet for them.
-Yeah, Jennifer Lopez.

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:25.457
-Chalamet came in
for a Sewer Boy?

00:05:25.457 --> 00:05:26.759
-Yeah.
-And he didn't get it?

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:28.995
-This close.
-They have the authenticity.

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:31.530
-He is very good.
-He read Fallon.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.198
-Oh, really?
-He's mad.

00:05:33.198 --> 00:05:34.833
That's why we're here.
-Oh, that's why you're here.

00:05:34.833 --> 00:05:36.269
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:36.269 --> 00:05:39.372
-There's some really -- So,
because, again, "Parent Trap,"

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:41.908
you then switch wigs,
or you switch hair.

00:05:41.908 --> 00:05:43.176
So, it's wigs.
-Yeah.

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:45.344
-And they're even, I will say --

00:05:45.344 --> 00:05:48.080
like, even Nathan's character
points out that the wigs --

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.715
it looks like a cheap wig.
-Yeah.

00:05:49.715 --> 00:05:51.450
-It does not feel like you
maybe spent a ton of money

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:54.387
on the wigs in this film.
Here you guys are with opposite.

00:05:54.387 --> 00:05:56.121
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.121 --> 00:05:58.791
So, I know this is both --
Look, this was a --

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:00.860
-Wait, could you even tell
that that was us playing?

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.162
-Who's to say?
-Could you even...

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:04.763
-Who is who?

00:06:04.763 --> 00:06:07.433
-Now, when did you --
It is very funny

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:08.867
because, obviously,
the first time you guys

00:06:08.867 --> 00:06:10.403
see each other in the movie,
you're taken aback

00:06:10.403 --> 00:06:12.270
by how you look identical
to one another.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:16.342
Do you remember the day when,
as young improvisers,

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:17.877
you first came up
with the idea of like,

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:20.913
"Wouldn't it be funny if we had
this as the jumping-off point?"

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:22.514
-Yeah, we were like, "For sure,
this will one day

00:06:22.514 --> 00:06:25.050
become a movie."
-Yes, this is the normal,

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:26.586
like, how most movies are made.

00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:29.222
You do them in the basement
of a grocery store,

00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:31.390
and then that's
"Kramer vs. Kramer."

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:33.659
-Yeah, that was
started that way.

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:35.027
-Have your parents
seen the film?

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:36.529
-They have.
-And?

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:38.798
-They're, like,
very lovely Southern people

00:06:38.798 --> 00:06:40.133
who you'd think
wouldn't like this,

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:42.001
and they love it.
And I think, furthermore,

00:06:42.001 --> 00:06:43.770
they love that we have
health insurance.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:45.604
-That's great.
-I think that is, like...

00:06:45.604 --> 00:06:48.107
[ Cheers and applause ]
-You guys deserve it.

00:06:48.107 --> 00:06:50.209
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:52.645
-And I also want to note
that the Sewer Boys,

00:06:52.645 --> 00:06:55.481
which we had here
and then we returned to A24,

00:06:55.481 --> 00:06:59.318
which made the film.
You wanted to keep --

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:01.153
-Oh, yes.
So, the last day of filming,

00:07:01.153 --> 00:07:02.888
everyone keeps
calling them grotesque.

00:07:02.888 --> 00:07:04.556
We find them to be beautiful.
-I don't see that.

00:07:04.556 --> 00:07:06.091
-I don't see --
I don't see that.

00:07:06.091 --> 00:07:08.127
And the last day of filming,
we had this whole plan.

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:09.495
We're like,
"We're gonna steal the puppets

00:07:09.495 --> 00:07:11.330
so we can keep them."
A24 caught us.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:12.764
They're like,
"You cannot do that.

00:07:12.764 --> 00:07:14.033
We need them for reshoots.

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:15.401
They might one day
go on 'Seth.'"

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:17.470
And then they said,
"We promise you,

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.672
when this movie cycle is done,
you will get them.

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:22.275
No one [Bleep] wants them
but you."

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:24.076
[ Laughter ]

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:26.779
-Well, it's nice to know
they're finally gonna end up

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:29.281
in a loving home.
-Gonna be administration.

00:07:29.281 --> 00:07:31.784
-Yes.
They're are assistants now.

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:33.151
-Guys, congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:07:33.151 --> 00:07:35.154
-It's so much fun
to watch a film

00:07:35.154 --> 00:07:37.489
that's unlike
anything I've ever seen.

00:07:37.489 --> 00:07:39.525
And it's great to have you both.
-Thank you.

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:41.227
-And they're doing --
We were asked to say

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:43.395
they're doing sing-alongs.
-Sing-along screening.

00:07:43.395 --> 00:07:45.564
-You guys are gonna do
sing-along screenings

00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:47.099
of the movie.
That's gonna start this week?

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:49.568
-This week, they're doing
with the words on the screen.

00:07:49.568 --> 00:07:51.637
This is up
for "Oppenheimer," too.

00:07:51.637 --> 00:07:52.738
-Yes, us and "Oppenheimer."
-They're gonna do

00:07:52.738 --> 00:07:54.507
a sing-along "Oppenheimer"?
-Yeah.

00:07:54.507 --> 00:07:56.342
-Great. They should.
Thank you guys so much

00:07:56.342 --> 00:07:58.344
for being here.
Aaron Jackson, Josh Sharp.

00:07:58.344 --> 00:07:59.779
"Dicks: The Musical"
is in theaters,

00:07:59.779 --> 00:08:01.180
and the soundtrack
is available now.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.682
Stick around. We'll be right
back with more "Late Night."

